Sports Injuries (Sprains/Strains)
Young Adult & Adolecence
Localized swelling, Pain on movement, Inability to bear weight, Laxity on stress testing

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: YAA-041
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe joint pain, massive swelling, and inability to ambulate safely following a high-impact sporting mechanism.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, Parent
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage revealed a patient who sustained an acute, high-velocity inversion injury to the ankle, resulting in immediate, agonizing pain, an audible 'pop', and a complete inability to weight-bear. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was strictly required to immediately rule out an unstable bi-malleolar fracture, assess for critical neurovascular compromise, and provide rapid joint immobilization and potent analgesia prior to the availability of the patient's regular GP.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I rolled my ankle outwards when I landed, heard a loud snap, and now the pain is unbearable and I can't put my foot on the ground."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a sudden onset of severe, localized joint pain in the right ankle, sustained 3 hours ago during a netball match. The patient vividly recalls an audible pop and snapping sensation at the moment of injury. This was immediately followed by rapid, massive localized soft tissue swelling and oedema over the lateral malleolus. The patient notes early ecchymosis tracking distally from the injury site towards the base of the fifth metatarsal. The patient reports a complete inability to fully bear weight, resulting in a profoundly antalgic gait when attempting to hop with support.
Functional Status: Bedbound; actively guarding the right lower extremity; entirely dependent on parents for mobility.
Social/Safety Concerns: High acute anxiety regarding a potential fracture requiring surgery and a prolonged absence from competitive sports.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 128/80 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Patient appears highly distressed, grimacing, and clutching the right lower leg.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal examination of the right ankle reveals severe, tense localized swelling and dependent oedema. There is exquisite localized point tenderness over the anterior talofibular ligament (ATFL) belly. Crucially, applying the Ottawa Ankle Rules, there is no bony tenderness along the posterior edge of the lateral or medial malleolus, the navicular, or the base of the fifth metatarsal, significantly lowering the pre-test probability of a fracture. Active and passive range of motion is severely restricted by pain and mechanical block from the swelling. Anterior drawer testing demonstrates palpable joint instability and laxity compared to the contralateral side. Neurovascular status is intact. The presentation strongly aligns with the diagnostic tetrad of localized swelling, pain on movement, inability to bear weight, and laxity on stress testing.
Environmental Observations: An ice pack is present but was applied ineffectively to the shin rather than the lateral ligaments; the leg is not elevated.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient took Ibuprofen 400mg 1 hour ago with minimal analgesic effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Grade II/III Lateral Ankle Sprain (ATFL Rupture) - Status: Acute Injury
Differentials:

Lateral Malleolus Fracture - Ruled out/unlikely due to the explicit lack of bony tenderness over the posterior 6cm of the fibula upon palpation (negative Ottawa Ankle Rules).

Achilles Tendon Rupture - Ruled out/unlikely due to the pain strictly localizing to the lateral ligamentous complex, a palpable and intact Achilles tendon, and a negative Thompson squeeze test.

Syndesmotic (High) Ankle Sprain - Ruled out/unlikely due to a negative squeeze test over the proximal fibula/tibia and no pain elicited upon external rotation stress of the foot.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low (Medical Risk: High risk of chronic instability if mismanaged)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient sustained a high-energy inversion injury resulting in immediate inability to bear weight, an audible pop, and massive localized swelling. The severity of the pain and the gross instability necessitated an immediate after-hours home visit to definitively rule out a surgical fracture and assess for vascular compromise from the swelling. Clinical examination utilizing the Ottawa Ankle Rules safely excluded a high likelihood of fracture, averting an unnecessary emergency department presentation. However, the confirmed ligamentous laxity required immediate rigid splinting and parenteral analgesia to stabilize the joint and control the severe acute inflammatory response, interventions that could not be delayed until the following day.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Ketorolac 30mg IM stat to rapidly suppress the acute inflammatory cascade and provide potent analgesia. Applied a rigid posterior backslab splint and a firm compression bandage to completely immobilize the ankle joint. Recommended a non-urgent plain radiograph via the regular GP to definitively exclude occult fracture, anticipating a normal radiograph with focal ligamentous disruption.
Positive Behavioral Change: To physically halt the massive accumulation of fluid in the joint, actively elevate the right ankle on three pillows so it rests definitively above the level of your heart for the next 48 hours, directly utilizing gravity to drain the oedema. To protect the torn ligaments from further catastrophic stretching, utilize the provided crutches for absolutely all ambulation, explicitly recognizing that any weight-bearing will rip the early scar tissue forming in the joint.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Fitted with axillary crutches and instructed on non-weight-bearing mobilization.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3-5 days to review the X-ray, remove the backslab, fit a functional CAM boot, and initiate an urgent referral to a sports physiotherapist.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department is required if the toes suddenly become cold, pale, or blue, if severe numbness develops in the foot, or if the calf muscle becomes tight, rigid, and agonizingly painful.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The clinical exam suggests a severe ligament tear rather than a broken bone, but a formal X-ray is required to definitively confirm this.
Clinical Course: The injected painkiller and the rigid splint will significantly reduce the sharp pain tonight, but the joint will remain severely swollen and bruised for several weeks, requiring intensive physiotherapy to regain stability.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Go to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if your toes turn blue or grey, if you lose feeling in your foot, or if your calf muscle suddenly becomes incredibly hard and painful.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The violent twist of your ankle has severely torn the ligaments that hold the outside of the joint together. The loud pop was the sound of that tissue tearing. The rigid splint and crutches are applied to completely lock the joint in place, preventing the torn ends of the ligament from separating further while they begin to heal.
Actionable Steps: Do not put a single ounce of weight on that foot under any circumstances. Keep the ankle elevated above your chest while lying down to stop blood from pooling and causing more throbbing pain.

SMS INFORMATION

Dx: Severe Ankle Sprain (Grade II/III). Splint and crutches applied. Given IM pain relief. Keep elevated. See regular GP in 3-5 days for X-ray and CAM boot fitting.

